<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;">I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Clémence Grislain*
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hamed Rahimi*
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>* Equal contribution</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Olivier Sigaud
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mohamed Chetouani
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institut des Systèmes Intelligents et de Robotique (ISIR), Sorbonne Université
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Paris, France
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{grislain, rahimi, sigaud, chetouani}@isir.upmc.fr</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Language-conditioned robotic manipulation in open-world settings requires not only accurate task execution but also the ability to detect failures for robust deployment in real-world environments. Although recent advances in vision-language models (VLMs) have significantly improved the spatial reasoning and task-planning capabilities of robots, they remain limited in their ability to recognize their own failures. In particular, a critical yet underexplored challenge lies in detecting semantic misalignment errors, where the robot executes a task that is semantically meaningful but inconsistent with the given instruction. To address this, we propose a method for building datasets targeting Semantic Misalignment Failures detection, from existing language-conditioned manipulation datasets. We also present <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I-FailSense</span>, an open-source VLM framework with grounded arbitration designed specifically for failure detection. Our approach relies on post-training a base VLM, followed by training lightweight classification heads, called <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FS blocks</span>, attached to different internal layers of the VLM and whose predictions are aggregated using an ensembling mechanism. Experiments show that I-FailSense outperforms state-of-the-art VLMs, both comparable in size and larger, in detecting semantic misalignment errors. Notably, despite being trained only on semantic misalignment detection, I-FailSense generalizes to broader robotic failure categories and effectively transfers to other simulation environments and real-world with zero-shot or minimal post-training.
The datasets and models are publicly released on HuggingFace<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        Please visit our <a href="https://clemgris.github.io/I-FailSense/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_bold">Webpage</a></span></span></span>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Humans and animals learn through failure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, a fundamental driver of intelligence and improvement.
For robotic systems, the ability to automatically evaluate and learn from failure is critical to achieve robust general-purpose manipulation in open-world settings.
Automatic failure evaluation has far-reaching implications: it enables reward shaping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, improves motion planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, facilitates sub-task verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and provides actionable feedback that enhances downstream robotic applications.
This capability is particularly important for foundation models for robotics, such as <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vision-Language-Action</em> (VLA) models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, which train policy networks to infer actions from multi-modal input consisting of visual observations and language instructions.
Within such foundation models, feedback-driven techniques, e.g. learning from human feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, have shown that iterative feedback loops can guide and improve performance.
However, a critical open question remains: <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">How can robotic foundation models autonomously detect failures without relying on external supervision?</em></p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16072v1/intro_fig13_failsense.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/365;" width="476" height="365" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of I-FailSense<span id="S1.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, which classifies a robot’s observation trajectories conditioned on language instructions into <span id="S1.F1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">failure</span> or <span id="S1.F1.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">success</span>. Trained on semantic misalignment failure detection, I-FailSense excels at identifying these challenging errors, zero-shot generalizes to detecting control errors and errors in new simulation environments, and detects errors in real-world observations with minimal post-training.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vision-Language Models</em> (VLMs) highlight their potential as building blocks for robotic agents, as they excel at multi-modal open-world reasoning, showing strong capabilities in instruction following, prediction, and spatial reasoning across images, video, audio, and time series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and are increasingly being applied in robotics for tasks such as object recognition, motion planning, spatial reasoning, and zero-shot trajectory generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
However, despite these advances, the detection of failures in robotic trajectories remains an open problem for off-the-shell VLMs. Previous work has introduced benchmarks and fine-tuning strategies for VLM-based failure detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, but these efforts primarily target <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">control errors</span>, e.g. the robot fails to grasp an object or drops it from the gripper. Far less attention has been given to <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semantic misalignment errors</span>, where the robot demonstrates a semantically meaningful behavior but that mismatches the given instruction. However, these failures highlight a fundamental limitation of VLMs, and by extension of robotic agents built upon them, which is the lack of grounding between visual observations, motion, and semantic instructions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
From a failure detection perspective, these errors also raise an important challenge as, unlike control errors which can be more easily identified from visual clues, semantic misalignment errors require aligning the instruction with both the spatial dimension of object-level interactions and the temporal dimension of the robot’s movements. Addressing this challenge requires models that integrate language grounding with trajectory reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce a new approach for the detection of language-conditioned robotic manipulation failure, with a focus on semantic misalignment errors, as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. To address this challenge, we build dedicated datasets from existing benchmarks and propose <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">I-FailSense</em>, a failure-aware extension of VLMs. Our method employs a two-stage post-training pipeline: (1) parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) of the base VLM with LoRA, followed by (2) freezing the VLM and training lightweight binary classification heads for failure detection, called <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">FailSense blocks</em> (FS blocks), over its internal language representations. Each block predicts the success or failure of the robot’s behavior in the observed trajectory with respect to its given instruction, and the final prediction is obtained by combining the outputs of the blocks through an arbitration mechanism. This design explicitly grounds failure detection in language-space reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, we demonstrate that I-FailSense outperforms zero-shot SOTA VLMs in detecting semantic misalignment errors reaching an accuracy of 90% in our proposed datasets built from simulated trajectories. Remarkably, I-FailSense generalizes beyond its training data and achieves strong performance on the AHA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, which includes (1) unseen simulated environments and (2) unseen error category (control errors), even surpassing the VLM-based baseline trained directly on these tasks by +19 points in accuracy. Furthermore, I-FailSense transfers to real-world semantic misalignment failure detection with minimal fine-tuning of the FS blocks, achieving 74% accuracy.
These results demonstrate the importance of designing architectures for robotic failure detection that effectively leverage the internal representations learned by VLMs. They also highlight that semantic misalignment failures are a particularly challenging and informative class of language-conditioned robotic manipulation errors.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16072v1/arch_failsense20.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">I-FailSense Architecture.<span id="S1.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (A) The model takes as input an observation trajectory aggregated into a single image <math id="S1.F2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times(H.N)\times(W.T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times(H.N)\times(W.T)}</annotation></semantics></math> (here, <math id="S1.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=1 PoV and <math id="S1.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>=4 timesteps) and a semantic goal <math id="S1.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and outputs a binary success/failure prediction <math id="S1.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. I-FailSense is built on a base VLM (PaliGemma2-mix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>) and is post-trained in two stages: (1) the projection MLP is fine-tuned along with LoRA adapters applied to the language modules of the VLM’s LLM base model, and (2) the VLM is frozen while the FS blocks, attached to the adapted language modules, are fine-tuned for binary classification. The FS block outputs are aggregated with the VLM’s final output through a voting mechanism to produce the final prediction. (B) The FS block architecture shows an hybrid attention pooling module composed of multi-head attention (MHA) and MLP followed by residual MLP blocks with batch normalization and ending in a binary classification MLP.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Failure Modes Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Failure detection and diagnosis are core problems in robotics. Early work in Human-Robot Interaction (HRI) and Task and Motion Planning (TAMP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> showed that transparent failure explanation is critical for trust in collaborative scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. These studies highlight the importance of not only detecting but also communicating failures. From the point of view of researchers and engineers, understanding the failures of an agent means understanding its shortcomings and is therefore crucial to improving it. In fact, efforts have been made to classify failures into fixed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> or learned <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> interpretable failure modes (e.g., <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wrong rotation</span>, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wrong grasp</span>, etc.). Recent work has also focused on building automated frameworks for failure analysis aimed at automatic policy improvement. Among these, AutoEval is a system for the autonomous evaluation of generalist robot policies that incorporates automatic success detection and scene resets, enabling scalable assessment in real-world settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. As another instance, the Robot Manipulation Diagnosis (RoboMD) framework employs deep reinforcement learning to actively explore and identify failure modes based on vision-language embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
Our work aligns with this literature as it tackles generalizable failure detection and includes a reflection on failure modes analysis. Yet most existing studies primarily target control errors, which can be explained by incorrect motions of the robot and a lack of physical control or precision. In contrast, we focus on semantic misalignment errors, which differ from control errors in that they are not caused by low-level mistakes but by incorrect high-level understanding:
They reveal that the agent fails to ground the given language instructions in its visual observations and motion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VLMs and LLMs for Failure Detection</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The integration of VLMs and LLMs has recently become a predominant paradigm for enhancing robotic manipulation, bringing a renewed focus to failure detection capabilities. A common approach has been to utilize off-the-shelf VLMs and LLMs as zero-shot or few-shot success detectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Some studies have adapted these models specifically for the task of failure detection through instruction-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Building on this progress, AHA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> represents a substantial advancement by formulating failure analysis as a generative, free-form reasoning task. This capability for rich explanatory reasoning facilitates integration with downstream systems for error recovery, leading to marked improvement in policy performance and demonstrating superiority over general-purpose models like GPT-4.
Our work, I-FailSense, builds directly upon the progress in VLM-based failure reasoning. While AHA powerfully demonstrated the value of generative explanations, its architecture is not explicitly optimized for the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">discriminative</span> task of high-accuracy failure classification—a prerequisite for triggering robust recovery mechanisms. In contrast, we decouple the explanatory power of a foundation VLM from a dedicated, trainable error classification head, ultimately achieving more accurate failure detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VLM Representations for Multimodal Reasoning</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Failure detection, and especially semantic misalignment error detection, can be viewed as a challenging instance of the visual grounding problem: the model must verify whether a semantic goal has been achieved in a visual input composed of multiple scenes where a robot interacts with its environment. Pre-trained VLMs learn rich cross-modal representations during training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> that can help address the visual grounding problem. However, although VLMs are trained on large internet-scale datasets for cross-modal tasks that require grounding textual instructions into visual inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, recent studies show that SOTA models still struggle with fine-grained visual grounding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and temporal/spatial reasoning about motion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under this perspective, prior works have explored enhancing VLMs by leveraging intermediate feature representations, often adding layers to extract and refine semantic information from middle layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. These refined representations can then be used for downstream robotic monitoring tasks, such as automatic failure classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> or detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Closer to our work, SAFE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> uses the features from the final layer of a VLA (built on a VLM) to predict control errors. In contrast, we exploit multiple levels of representation and aggregate predictions from those representations using a voting mechanism—a widely used strategy for improving both robustness and accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>–to detect control errors and semantic misalignment errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminary</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Statement</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The problem of robotic manipulation from language instruction and visual observations can be formulated as a goal-conditioned Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(S,A,\mathcal{T},\rho_{0},\Omega,O,G)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(S,A,\mathcal{T},\rho_{0},\Omega,O,G),</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the state space, with the initial state sampled from the distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The robot receives partial observations through the observation function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to its visual sensors. With <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> camera point of views (PoV), the observation is represented as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(s)\in\Omega=\mathbb{R}^{3\times H\times W\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(s)\in\Omega=\mathbb{R}^{3\times H\times W\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> are the image height and width. The robot selects actions in the action space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> (such as joint configurations or
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">n-DoF</span> commands) conditioned on observation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(s)</annotation></semantics></math> and a textual goal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G\subseteq\mathcal{V}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G\subseteq\mathcal{V}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vocabulary and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> all finite sequences over <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. The transition function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> governs the environment dynamics.
Given this formulation, we define a robot trajectory of size <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> as a sequence of observations, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(o_{0}\dots,o_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(o_{0}\dots,o_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this work, we are interested in the binary classification of trajectories into <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">success</span> of <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">failure</span> with respect to the textual goal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Failure Modes in Robotic Manipulation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Robotic failures can arise from different factors that reflect different shortcomings. In Duan et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, robotic manipulation failures are decomposed into seven categories. Among these, six are attributed to <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">control errors</span> (e.g., incomplete grasp, incorrect rotation, etc.), while only one (Wrong Target Object) corresponds to a misalignment between the language instruction and the action executed by the agent, e.g. the robot interacts with the wrong object. Yet, such semantic errors are not limited to wrong-object failures and can extend to broader forms of <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semantic misalignment errors</span> where the agent behavior mismatches the language goal instruction. Whereas the other categories in AHA characterize low-level control mistakes, this type of failure highlights a lack of semantic grounding of the agent, a challenge that has been well documented in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Detecting such errors is therefore crucial for improving the grounding capabilities of robotic agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, unlike control errors, which can often be detected without semantic reasoning (e.g., simply observing that the robot failed to grasp any object), identifying semantic misalignment errors requires deeper understanding of the relations between textual instructions and observation trajectories. As shown in the examples in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ V-A Datasets ‣ V Experimental Setup ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, detecting such failures requires reasoning not only about the object being manipulated but also about the robot’s spatial movements over time, therefore requiring grounding the textual instruction in the spatial and temporal dimensions of the observation trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Method</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus on detecting language-conditioned robotic manipulation failures, with an emphasis on semantic misalignment errors. We introduce datasets targeting this challenging failure detection problem (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Semantic Misalignment Failure Datasets ‣ IV Method ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a>), and propose a new method for post-training a VLM agent and leveraging its internal representation for failure detection (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B FailSense: Vision-Language Model for Failure Detection ‣ IV Method ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>). The overall architecture is presented in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semantic Misalignment Failure Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first contribution is a way to construct datasets <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF}}</annotation></semantics></math> focusing on <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>emantic <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>isalignment <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ailures. To this end, we leverage existing multi-task, language-conditioned robotic manipulation datasets that provide expert demonstrations <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau,g^{*}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau,g^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the semantic goal <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is successfully achieved in trajectory <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. From any expert demonstration dataset, we define <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF}}=\{\tau,g,y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF}}=\{\tau,g,y\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the observation trajectory, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> the textual instruction, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> a binary success label. Half of the examples are positive (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>), while the other half are negative (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G\setminus\{g^{*}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G\setminus\{g^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Positive examples:</span> These correspond to the expert trajectories paired with their original textual instructions <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau,g^{*},y=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau,g^{*},y=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negative examples:</span> To create negative examples for semantic misalignment, we take existing expert trajectories and pair them with textual instructions different from the original one, but belonging to the same task category (e.g., <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lifting</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pushing</span>, etc.). These negative examples are challenging in two ways: (1) the agent demonstrates behavior that is semantically meaningful but that does not correspond to the paired instruction, thereby characterizing semantic misalignment failures; (2) the paired instruction and the original one belong to the same task type, making the distinction subtle (e.g., ”lift the red block” vs. ”lift the blue block,” or ”rotate the red block right” vs. ”rotate the red block left”).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FailSense: Vision-Language Model for Failure Detection</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our second contribution is a post-training approach that adapts a pretrained VLM, PaliGemma2-mix-3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, for robotic failure detection. We select this base VLM for its good trade-off between its size (3B parameters) and performance. Our method, illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, follows a two-stage training: first, we post-train the VLM on the failure detection task using LoRA (<a href="#S4.SS2.SSS1" title="IV-B1 Supervised Fine-Tuning (Stage 1) ‣ IV-B FailSense: Vision-Language Model for Failure Detection ‣ IV Method ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B1</span></a>); second, we introduce our novel classification heads called <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FailSense (FS) blocks</span>, which are attached to multiple layers of the post-trained VLM and whose outputs are combined using an ensembling method for robust failure detection (Section <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS2" title="IV-B2 Grounded arbitration (Stage 2) ‣ IV-B FailSense: Vision-Language Model for Failure Detection ‣ IV Method ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>1 </span>Supervised Fine-Tuning (Stage 1)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We post-train the VLM by fine-tuning the MLP layer between the frozen vision encoder (SigLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>) and the base language model. Simultaneously, to achieve parameter-efficient adaptation of the language component, we incorporate Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> modules into the key-query-value (KQV) projection layers of attention blocks of the language model. This strategy introduces a small number of task-specific trainable parameters while keeping the majority of the pretrained model weights frozen.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The VLM processes a multi-modal input consisting of an observation trajectory <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and a textual instruction <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The trajectory <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is aggregated into a single image representation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times(H\cdot N)\times(W\cdot T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times(H\cdot N)\times(W\cdot T)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of point of views and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the number of observations. The combined input is formatted through a fixed prompt template <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\tau,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\tau,g)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then fed into the model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For supervised training, we minimize the cross-entropy loss between the distribution predicted by the model and the target supervision token (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;success&gt;</span> or <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;fail&gt;</span>):</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(\theta)=-\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M}\log p_{\theta}\left(t_{i}\mid x,t_{&lt;i}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(\theta)=-\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M}\log p_{\theta}\left(t_{i}\mid x,t_{&lt;i}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the sequence length, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the target token at position <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{&lt;i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{&lt;i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents all previous tokens, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the input context (i.e. the prompt), and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability distribution of the model with parameters <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, over the vocabulary.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>2 </span>Grounded arbitration (Stage 2)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To leverage the diverse intermediate representations learned by the VLM for failure detection, we attach classification heads to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> layers, indexed by <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are evenly distributed across the depth of the base language model. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, each head takes as input features from a different depth of the model. At inference time, the outputs of all heads are aggregated to produce the final prediction, allowing binary classification that accounts for varying levels of reasoning abstraction. All classification heads are randomly initialized but share the same architecture. During this second training phase, only the FS blocks are updated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FailSense blocks:</span>
The architecture of our classification heads is shown on the right of Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Each head takes the features from its corresponding post-trained VLM layer <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k}=VLM_{\theta^{\prime}}(P(\tau,g))[i_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k}=VLM_{\theta^{\prime}}(P(\tau,g))[i_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math> of the LLM block, and aggregates them using a hybrid attention pooling mechanism that combines MLP and Multi-Head Attention (MHA). The aggregated features are then processed through a sequence of MLP blocks with residual connections and batch normalization. A final binary classification linear layer predicts the binary probability <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}=FS_{\phi_{k}}(f_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}=FS_{\phi_{k}}(f_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. At training time, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated based on the binary cross entropy between the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th head prediction <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the true label <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Voting Strategy:</span>
At inference, each FS block outputs a probability distribution <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is converted into a binary prediction <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}=\arg\max(p_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}=\arg\max(p_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. We also convert the free-form output of the VLM into a binary prediction <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{vlm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{vlm}</annotation></semantics></math>. The final prediction <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed using a weighted voting mechanism:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{y}=\mathds{1}\Bigg[\sum_{k=1}^{K}\omega_{k}\,y_{k}+\omega_{vlm}y_{vlm}&gt;0.5\left(\sum_{k=1}^{K}\omega_{k}+\omega_{vlm}\right)\Bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\mathds{1}\Bigg[\sum_{k=1}^{K}\omega_{k}\,y_{k}+\omega_{vlm}y_{vlm}&gt;0.5\left(\sum_{k=1}^{K}\omega_{k}+\omega_{vlm}\right)\Bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the weight of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th FS block and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{vlm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{vlm}</annotation></semantics></math> of the VLM prediction. In practice, we use <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=3</annotation></semantics></math>, assign equal weights to the FS blocks predictions (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) and give the VLM prediction double weight (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{vlm}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{vlm}=2</annotation></semantics></math>) to break ties.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Setup</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train I-FailSense on a semantic misalignment dataset, built following the pipeline presented in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B FailSense: Vision-Language Model for Failure Detection ‣ IV Method ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>. We design experiments addressing the following research questions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can I-FailSense reliably detect semantic misalignment errors?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Does this capability transfer to detecting control errors?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How well does I-FailSense generalize to failure detection on out-of-distribution (OOD) simulated trajectories?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q4</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How well does it generalize to failure detection on real-world
?</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we conduct an ablation study to evaluate the contribution of each training stage in our pipeline to the overall performance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SMF-CALVIN dataset:</span>
To address <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we build a semantic misalignment failure dataset <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the CALVIN task_D benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. This benchmark comprises 34 simulated robotic manipulation tasks, which we grouped into six categories: <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lifting</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rotating</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pushing</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">opening/closing</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">placing</span>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lighting</span>. For each task, the benchmark provides around 150 expert demonstrations collected via teleoperation and labeled with a textual instruction corresponding to the task. Each task includes 11 different textual instructions to increase semantic complexity. Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ V-A Datasets ‣ V Experimental Setup ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows an example of a positive and a negative example.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16072v1/smf_dataset.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/193;" width="476" height="193"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Example data in <math id="S5.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Top: a positive example where the observation trajectory correctly matches the paired instruction. Bottom: a negative example illustrating semantic misalignment, where the robot rotates the correct object—the pink cube—right instead of the instructed left.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AHA dataset:</span>
To address both <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q3</annotation></semantics></math>, we evaluate I-FailSense trained on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math>, on the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. This failure detection benchmark is built on RLBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> and comprises 79 different simulated tasks. <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math> defines seven error categories: six control errors (incomplete grasp, inadequate grip retention, misaligned keyframe, incorrect rotation, missing rotation, and wrong action sequence) and one semantic misalignment error (wrong target object). Failures in these categories are generated heuristically from RLBench as detailed in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AHA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AHA</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Observation trajectories include both exocentric and egocentric PoV. Since the dataset is not publicly released, we constructed a test set of 400 negative trajectory–instruction pairs following the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AHA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AHA</annotation></semantics></math> pipeline; two representative examples (exocentric PoV) are illustrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ V-A Datasets ‣ V Experimental Setup ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16072v1/aha_dataset1.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/190;" width="476" height="190"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Example data in <math id="S5.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Two negative examples from the AHA dataset (exocentric PoV) demonstrating control failures—top: the knife slips through the robot’s gripper; bottom: the robot fails to grasp the computer lid.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SMF-DROID dataset:</span>
To address <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q4</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a second semantic misalignment failure dataset, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-DROID</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}</annotation></semantics></math>, derived from the DROID dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. DROID consists of real-world robot demonstrations recorded from multiple PoV: one egocentric (gripper) and two exocentric cameras. In <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-DROID</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}</annotation></semantics></math>, these demonstrations serve as positive examples. To generate negative examples, we use the pipeline described in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Semantic Misalignment Failure Datasets ‣ IV Method ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> and pair each trajectory in DROID with a randomly mismatched instruction, considering only one task category. The resulting dataset <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-DROID</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6K</annotation></semantics></math> training and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="276" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>276</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">276</annotation></semantics></math> test data, with half positive and half negative trajectory–instruction pairs (see Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ V-A Datasets ‣ V Experimental Setup ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16072v1/droid_fig1.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/167;" width="476" height="167"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Example data in <math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">SMF-DROID</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Two examples from the semantic misalignment failure dataset built on DROID (exocentric PoV)–top: a positive example where the instruction matches the observation trajectory; bottom: a negative example where the instruction and trajectory mismatch.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baselines and Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines:</span> We compare I-FailSense against several SOTA zero-shot VLMs from different model families, including models of comparable sizes (I-FailSense base model PaliGemma2-mix-3B) as well as larger ones (Qwen2.5-VL-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>). For evaluation on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we additionally report results of the AHA model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. The AHA model shares the same input format as I-FailSense, i.e. the observation trajectory flattened into a single image <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times(H.N)\times(W.T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times(H.N)\times(W.T)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its fine-tuning procedure also closely parallels the first training stage of I-FailSense: the model is initialized from LLaMA-2-13B, with fine-tuning applied only to the projector and transformer weights, while using a frozen vision encoder and tokenizer, following the pipeline introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics:</span>
For evaluation on datasets composed of both positive and negative examples (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-DROID</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-DROID}}</annotation></semantics></math>), we use standard binary classification metrics, which are accuracy, precision (true positives over predicted positives), recall (true positives over real positives), and F1 score.
For evaluation on <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which only contains negative examples, we report accuracy, which can be interpreted as a failure detection rate.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results</span></h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semantic Misalignment Failure Detection (Q1)</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the performance of I-FailSense in detecting semantic misalignment failures, we tested it on the set of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math> tests. These data are in-distribution both in terms of error type (semantic misalignment failures) and environment (CALVIN). Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table I ‣ VI-A Semantic Misalignment Failure Detection (Q1) ‣ VI Results ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> presents the results of I-FailSense after one training phase, labeled <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(LoRA only)</span> and the full training, labeled <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ours)</span>, compared to zero-shot VLMs. The results are shown when using one exocentric PoV (<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=1) or an exocentric and egocentric PoV (<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=2).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S6.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
