<td id="S6.T1.3.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T1.3.11.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBEB;">0.8818</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.3.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T1.3.11.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBEB;">0.8596</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.3.11.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T1.3.11.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBEB;">0.9245</span></td>
<td id="S6.T1.3.11.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T1.3.11.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBEB;">0.8909</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S6.T1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Evaluation metrics of I-FailSense on <math id="S6.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math> after Stage 1 <span id="S6.T1.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(LoRA only)</span> and Stages 1 + 2 <span id="S6.T1.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ours)</span>, compared to zero-shot VLMs, for failure detection from observation trajectories captured from one (exocentric) or two PoV (exocentric and egocentric). Best results are shown in bold.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can see in Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table I ‣ VI-A Semantic Misalignment Failure Detection (Q1) ‣ VI Results ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> that zero-shot GPT-4o and PaliGemma2-mix (I-FailSense’s base model) fail to detect semantic misalignment errors from observation trajectories, performing close to random no matter they use only the exocentric PoV or both exocentric and egocentric PoV. Zero-shot Qwen2.5-VL performs better, reaching 68% with a single PoV and 69% with two PoV. I-FailSense outperforms all the zero-shot VLMs, achieving 90% accuracy with a single PoV (exocentric) and 88% with both PoV.
Qwen2.5-VL achieves a recall of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.92</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.92</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.87" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.87</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.87</annotation></semantics></math>, but only a precision of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.63</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.63</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.65" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.65</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.65</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively using one and two PoV. This suggests that while the model correctly identifies successful trajectories with respect to the instruction, it frequently misclassifies misaligned trajectories as successful (i.e., false positives). This behavior arises from the semantic misalignment failure dataset, which is designed with challenging negative examples where differences between the robot’s behavior in the trajectory and the paired instruction are subtle (see Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Semantic Misalignment Failure Datasets ‣ IV Method ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a>). While I-FailSense still exhibits this precision-recall gap, it is reduced to <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.06</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.06</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.07</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.07</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in F1 scores of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.91" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.91</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.91</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.89" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.89</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.89</annotation></semantics></math>, compared to <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen2.5-VL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generalization Across Error Types (Q2)</span></h3>

<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16072v1/aha_results12.svg" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:486/288;" width="486" height="288"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Failure detection rate of I-FailSense trained on <math id="S6.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluated on <math id="S6.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math>, compared to <math id="S6.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AHA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AHA</annotation></semantics></math> baselines (7B, 13B) trained on <math id="S6.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math> and zero-shot VLMs. All models use both egocentric and exocentric PoV. Results from prior work are marked with *.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ VI-B Generalization Across Error Types (Q2) ‣ VI Results ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, zero-shot VLMs perform poorly on failure detection in the <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AHA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AHA</annotation></semantics></math> dataset. GPT-4o achieves near-random accuracy, while PaliGemma2-mix-3B (base-model of I-FailSense) and Qwen2.5-VL perform even below random, likely due to the inherent bias of some VLMs for predicting success rather than failure and/or the simulated nature of the dataset. When fine-tuned on failure detection, the AHA models reach 69% and 70% accuracy for the 7B and 13B models. I-FailSense outperforms both, reaching <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="89\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>89</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">89\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy, even though it was trained only on semantic misalignment errors, while <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math> mostly contains control errors (<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>85%) and only a small fraction (<math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>15%) of semantic misalignment errors (which is limited to wrong-object cases). This indicates that learning about semantic misalignment failures also provides strong signals to detect control errors. This can be explained by the fact that, when trained on semantic misalignment errors, the model learns to align language instructions with observation trajectories and to verify whether the robot’s motion matches the instruction (i.e. interacting with the right object in the right way). In misalignment errors, the robot performs a meaningful but incorrect motion (e.g. opening a drawer instead of the slider), while in control errors it fails to perform a meaningful motion at all (e.g. failing to grasp or dropping an object). Since both cases require checking whether the observed motion matches the instruction, training on semantic misalignment errors naturally transfers to control error detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generalization to OOD Simulations Trajectories (Q3)</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The strong performance of I-FailSense on the <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AHA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AHA</annotation></semantics></math> dataset (Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ VI-B Generalization Across Error Types (Q2) ‣ VI Results ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) demonstrates its ability to generalize across simulation environments. Specifically, <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises trajectories from the RLBench environment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, where a Franka arm interacts with varied objects (e.g., knifes, bowls, drawers etc.) relevant for the task, on a table (Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ V-A Datasets ‣ V Experimental Setup ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). In contrast, I-FailSense was trained on <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math>, derived from the CALVIN environment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, which also features a Franka arm (Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ V-A Datasets ‣ V Experimental Setup ‣ I-FailSense: Towards General Robotic Failure Detection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) but with a different and fixed set of objects (e.g., cube, lights, slider etc.). Furthermore, observations are captured from different camera settings. The good performance of I-FailSense on <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>AHA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{AHA}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the model has learned on <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>SMF-CALVIN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{SMF-CALVIN}}</annotation></semantics></math> failure detection patterns that transfer effectively to other simulated environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
